1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:08,440
December 31 at four o'clock: the income statement prints 2 billion VND in net profit, yet Account 112 holds only 50 million VND against 500 million due for staff payroll.

2
00:00:08,440 --> 00:00:09,690
Where did all the cash go?

3
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:17,360
Under accrual accounting, shipping a 10 billion VND wholesale order puts all 10 billion revenue on the books right away.

4
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:22,010
Yet the corporate buyer wired 7.5 billion cash and deferred 2.5 billion for sixty days.

5
00:00:22,760 --> 00:00:35,650
Count pure bank cash moving across the quarter: 1 billion starting cash plus 7.5 billion collected equals 8.5 billion in, minus 7.45 billion wired for stock and 1 billion overhead, leaving only 50 million.

6
00:00:36,450 --> 00:00:47,810
Not a single dong was stolen: 2.5 billion sits inside unpaid customer invoices on Account 131, and 950 million sits stacked inside cardboard boxes on warehouse shelves under Account 156.

7
00:00:48,570 --> 00:00:59,970
Subtracting those locked working capital balances turns +2 billion VND in paper profit into negative 950 million VND in Operating Cash Flow across the entire quarter.

8
00:01:00,650 --> 00:01:08,980
To clear 500 million in salaries thirty minutes before cutoff, the director borrows 450 million short-term at 10.5% interest.

9
00:01:08,980 --> 00:01:13,650
Book profit fills annual reports; real cash on Account 112 pays staff.
